Maintainer: Ken Kelley <kkelley@nd.edu>
Description: Procedures for planning a study whose sample size cannot be
    fixed in advance, because the population values that a sample size
    formula needs are unknown. The 'SMSD' package implements accuracy in
    parameter estimation (AIPE) and minimum risk point estimation
    (MRPE) procedures that collect data in stages and decide whether
    to keep sampling based on the data so far, along with a budget
    constrained sequential procedure for K populations. The methods
    are general; the cited articles develop them in the context of
    psychology, sociology, education, management, marketing, and
    information systems. The methods implemented here are developed
    in Chattopadhyay and Kelley (2016) <doi:10.1080/00273171.2016.1203279>,
    Chattopadhyay and Kelley (2017) <doi:10.1037/met0000089>, Kelley,
    Darku, and Chattopadhyay (2018) <doi:10.1037/met0000127>, Kelley,
    Darku, and Chattopadhyay (2019) <doi:10.1037/met0000203>,
    Chattopadhyay, Bandyopadhyay, Kelley, and Padalunkal (2025)
    <doi:10.1037/met0000570>, and Chattopadhyay and De (2016)
    <doi:10.3390/econometrics4030030>, with foundational treatment in
    Mukhopadhyay and de Silva (2009, ISBN:9780367386535). The 'SMSD'
    package complements the 'MBESS' and 'DMAR' packages (Kelley, 2007,
    <doi:10.3758/BF03192993>) for fixed sample size analysis and the
    'BUCSS' package (Anderson, Kelley, and Maxwell, 2017,
    <doi:10.1177/0956797617723724>) for bias and uncertainty corrected
    sample size planning.
